.TH CLEAR 1 "2026-05-15" "User Commands" "CLI-Improved Manual"
.SH NAME
clear \- clear the terminal screen and purge scrollback buffer
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B clear
.SH DESCRIPTION
A high-fidelity replacement for the standard \fBclear\fR utility.
.PP
Standard \fBclear\fR only moves the terminal cursor to the top line of the 
window, leaving historical command output intact within the scrollback buffer. 
This refined version visually clears the screen and immediately executes hardware-level 
ANSI escape sequences to flush the terminal emulator's scrollback memory entirely.
.SH THE FUNCTION
This manual entry documents the following shell function and alias configuration:
.IP
.nf
clear() {
    /usr/bin/clear
    printf '\\033c\\033[3J'
}
alias cls='clear'
.fi
.SH ESCAPE SEQUENCES
The tool relies on the following low-level terminal controls via \fBprintf\fR:
.TP
\fB\\033c\fR
Resets the terminal device state to its default settings.
.TP
\fB\\033[3J\fR
Purges the entire visible and invisible scrollback history from the host terminal.
.SH ADVANTAGES
.IP \(bu 3
\fBCognitive Reset:\fR Guarantees a truly blank canvas when shifting contexts, 
preventing accidental scrolling into stale data.
.IP \(bu 3
\fBError Tolerance:\fR Intercepts the Windows-style \fBcls\fR command to maintain 
workflow velocity across heterogeneous environments.
.SH INSTALLATION
Add the function and alias directly to your \fB.bashrc\fR or \fB.functions\fR file. 
Because it is loaded as a shell function, it naturally shadows the standard 
binary located at \fB/usr/bin/clear\fR.
